WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.514
<v Instructor>Okay, so now let's talk about DocumentDB.</v>

2
00:00:02.514 --> 00:00:03.870
So we know Aurora.

3
00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.570
And Aurora, I call it an AWS implementation

4
00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:07.854
of PostgreSQL, or MySQL

5
00:00:07.854 --> 00:00:09.990
because it has the same API,

6
00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:10.935
but it's built on AWS,

7
00:00:10.935 --> 00:00:14.340
and it has some nice benefits to be cloud native.

8
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:19.260
Now, DocumentDB is the same as Aurora, but for MongoDB.

9
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.480
So MongoDB is a NoSQL database,

10
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.170
and it's used to store query and index JSON data.

11
00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.090
So thanks to DocumentDB, well, we have

12
00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.127
this similar deployment concept as Aurora.

13
00:00:29.127 --> 00:00:31.620
It's going to be fully managed, highly available,

14
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.123
with replication across 3 AZ,

15
00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.120
and the DocumentDB storage layer

16
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.730
will just like Aurora grow automatically

17
00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:40.890
in increments of 10 gigabytes.

18
00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.440
Finally, DocumentDB is going to be scaling

19
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.050
to or close with millions of requests per second.

20
00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.330
So it makes it a much nicer

21
00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:50.310
cloud native alternative to MongoDB,

22
00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.040
if you're using MongoDB on AWS.

23
00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.170
So you pay for what you use in DocumentDB

24
00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:56.970
and there is no upfront costs,

25
00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.760
but you need to understand the architecture of DocumentDB,

26
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.620
which is very similar to Aurora,

27
00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.150
in order to understand its pricing.

28
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.860
So we have database storage,

29
00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.230
and then we have on-demand instances,

30
00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:08.430
we have primary instances

31
00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:11.790
and then replica instances, that's our app.

32
00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.797
So you're going to pay for these on-demand instances,

33
00:01:13.797 --> 00:01:18.030
you're going to pay per second with a minimum of 10 minutes.

34
00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.190
Then these instances are going to do a read

35
00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:22.617
and write operations against your database storage.

36
00:01:22.617 --> 00:01:25.560
So you're going to pay for the database IO

37
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.770
and it's going to be counted per million iOS.

38
00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.660
Then of course, the data is being stored

39
00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:31.747
on your database storage, and therefore,

40
00:01:31.747 --> 00:01:34.950
you're going to pay for the database storage itself

41
00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.870
in gigabytes per month.

42
00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.470
And finally, if you do backups of DocumentDB,

43
00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:42.120
then these backups are going to be backed

44
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:43.640
internally into Amazon S3,

45
00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:45.870
and so therefore you will also have a cost

46
00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.840
per gigabyte per month for these backups.

47
00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:50.790
So DocumentDB is all provision.

48
00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.850
There is no on demand tier of DocumentDB.

49
00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.530
This is a misconception.

50
00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:57.120
So you pay for your instances,

51
00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.330
your iOS, your storage and your backup.

52
00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:01.260
That's it for this lecture.

53
00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:02.460
I hope you liked it.

54
00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.503
And I will see you in the next lecture.

